you know what sometimes you just need a  gaming headset that works one with good  quality sound a  decent microphone and isn't gonna break  the bank with all those extra bells and  whistles which is exactly what we're  going to be looking at today we have two  of the biggest names in video game  hardware razer and hyperx and we're  going to be taking a look at their  cheapest headphone offerings the razer  kraken x and the hyperx cloud stinger  both of which run for an msrp of fifty  dollars although it's worth pointing out  we were able to find both of these for  only forty dollars i'll say right now  that both of these are pretty solid  choices if you're looking for headphones  within this budget range but the  question is  which one is better let's start with the  razer kraken x it is pretty much a  paired down version of the flagship  kraken series in every way the frame is  made out of plastic which gives it a  very light feel  like extremely light like almost cheap  feeling and how light it is now granted  your feelings on this are going to be  almost entirely subjective the actual  build quality on it is solid and it does  feel noticeably less present when on  your head it's just a matter of whether  you prefer headsets with a heavier feel  or not if the lighter feel is what  you're looking for then points to the  kraken still i can't i can't get over  how light it is like i i know that's  part of the selling point is that it's  like it's really light but it's just  i don't know it needs more heft compared  to the more expensive kraken models  which offer cooling gel layer and heat  transfer fabric these are just your  standard pleather cushions although they  do have some memory foam inside and they  do feel nice they're pretty thick but  with an airy inside and i quite like the  feel of them i've used them for several  work days in a row and i found them  comfortable throughout as i mentioned  these are budget headphones so don't  expect any of the usual fancy features  you get with razer no rgb lighting or  anything like that what you do get is a  mute button and a volume slider on the  left headphone a cable extension that  splits it out into audio and microphone  and that's pretty much it you just you  plug it in and it  it works  so i am no audiophile but to my average  sounding ears these are pretty decent  quality headphones for the price range  i've tested them on the pc the xbox the  switch ps5 and they all sounded pretty  good i found the positional audio to be  pretty solid when playing multiplayer  games like destiny 2 and overall i i  didn't really have any issues with them  now as a bonus the kraken x does come  with an activation code for razer's 7.1  surround sound software it's designed to  simulate the positional audio provided  by 7.1 surround sound in order to  quote  give you a competitive advantage i don't  know about that but it is a neat little  thing and when i was flipping back and  forth i could tell a little bit of a  difference keep in mind that this  doesn't mean these are actual true 7.1  surround sound headphones it's simply  the software emulating that to the best  of its ability and these aren't gonna  sound as good as true expensive 7.1  surround sound headphones so don't go in  thinking that one pretty cool thing  though is that once you download it you  can technically use this software with  any pair of headphones so the last thing  to talk about is the microphone it's a  short  bendable microphone that stays out of  the way of your face in fact most of the  time i kind of stopped perceiving it was  even there because of how small it is  which is exactly what i like in a  headset microphone i i don't like the  ones where you just they're clunky and  in their way and you're just constantly  thinking about it so i i find that you  just sort of ignore this one with the  only thing that kind of sucks about it  is that it doesn't retract at all  meaning you just sort of have to bend it  up when you don't want to use it it  doesn't look that great to its credit  because of how small it is i again i  just sort of stopped even perceiving  that it was there and and after a while  it's fine but if you're looking for  something that kind of like looks nice  when you want to put it away uh this is  it's not gonna offer that as for the  sound quality you're not gonna be  getting anything amazing but it will  come through clearly on discord which  is what you want here's a sample of the  microphone from my producer richard  this is the razer kraken x one two three  four five okay let's move on to the  cloud  stinger  now compared to the kraken x this has a  much heavier weight thanks to the metal  bar in the bridge i quite like the feel  of it it also offers these 90 degree  rotations on the headphones this is a  feature that i've never really cared for  on headphones i just kind of find them  more annoying to hold if anything else  but when you're actually wearing them  the headphones themselves are very  stable very comfortable i do quite like  how it feels overall it is worth  pointing out that at least on mine the  right headphone does make a squeaky  sound when you rotate it  now compared to the kraken x the  cushions are a little bit thinner and  denser just a different style of design  for the cushion but i would say the  comfort level is pretty comparable to  each other the biggest difference to me  is that the ear hole is slightly larger  so they covered my ears better so if you  have large ears these might be the  preferable option now feature wise these  are pretty bare bones you get the same  extension cable that you get with the  kraken x and they have the volume slider  although it is a noticeably lower  quality volume slider it just kind of  feels like two pieces of plastic rubbing  against each other  yeah not great  the audio quality is on par with the  kraken x i tested it with all the same  games on all the same consoles and to my  untrained ears they pretty much sounded  exactly the same the cloud stinger  doesn't come with any sort of extra  software the way the kraken x does but  yes i did test it on the razer 7.1  surround sound software and it did make  a difference and once again we end with  the headphones it is a much thicker  design than the kraken x although it is  still relatively out of the way in the  kind of way that i like it also has a  little adjustable bend on the tip which  is good  and it has a nice little nook at the top  to tuck itself away when you're done  with it which is way better than the  awkward kraken x design where you're  just kind of bending it that said in our  testing with the cloud stinger the mic  quality is on par with the kraken x's  this is the hyperx cloud stinger testing  one two three four five now you may have  noticed when you fold it up and down it  actually makes a clicking sound and that  is the microphone muting and then  unmuting i mean you know when you're  flipping it up like this you aren't  going to be talking anymore so naturally  you want it to mute itself but at the  same time i kind of wish they had the  button the way the kraken x did  especially if you just need to mute  yourself for a second instead you kind  of have to like physically move it up  and down then everyone's going to kind  of hear that that muffling and scraping  as you're physically moving it  i can't imagine that sounds all that  great so  decision time  at the end of the day i found these  headphones to be pretty comparable to  each other and i had little nitpicks  with each one but  honestly they're really really close but  if i had to pick  i'd probably go with the razer kraken x  even though it's a lot lighter than i'm  used to with headphones and the  microphone it kind of just awkwardly  sits there i actually do like the  smaller size of the microphone overall i  prefer the mute button on the back and i  think i actually like the memory foam in  the cushions a little bit better really  though these are just personal  preferences maybe you prefer the weight  and the out of the way nature of the  headphones in the cloud stinger  hopefully i gave you all the information  you need to decide which of these two is  right for you if you're interested in  either of these headsets we have links  for them in the description below and  hey you stayed all the way to the end of  this video so why not hit the like and  sub button if you haven't already we've  got tons of great hardware videos here  at gamespot such as michael heim's  recent review of the xbox wireless  headset so why not go check that out  thanks for watching  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

